HTTP Parameter Pollution is a technique where you inject arbitrary parameters into HTTP requests to bypass input validation and WAF rules. It's commonly used in automated testing, but it creates a false impression of security [3.3] because it mimics real traffic patterns.

Here's a minimal example using the HttpWebRequest class:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Net.Http.Formatting;
using System.Net.Http.Headers;
using System.Net.Http.Json;
using System.Net.Http.Headers.Formatting;

public class HttpParameterPoleViper
{
    public static void Test(string url)
    {
        var request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, url);
        var response = request.GetResponse();

        // Add parameters to the request
        var headers = new HttpHeaders();
        headers.Add("User-Agent", "MyApp");
        headers.Add("Accept-Language", "en-US");
        headers.Add("Accept-Encoding", "gzip");

        // Add headers to the response
        response.Headers.Add("User-Agent", "MyApp");
        response.Headers.Add("Accept-Language", "en-US");
        response.Headers.Add("Accept-Encoding", "gzip");

        // Add parameters to the response
        response.Headers.Add("User-Agent", "MyApp");
        response.Headers.Add("Accept-Language", "en-US");
        response.Headers.Add("Accept-Encoding", "gzip");

        // Send the request
        var responseStream = request.GetResponseStream();
        responseStream.Write(response.GetResponseStream(), 0, response.ContentLength);
    }
}
```

This works because the `GetResponseStream()` method returns a stream that can be read directly. However, modern WAFs (Web Application Firewalls) and Content Security Policies (CSP) are designed to block this pattern. If you're building a legitimate test suite, you'd typically use a dedicated tool like OWASP ZAP or Burp Suite Pro to capture the traffic and inspect the headers.

For production, consider using a WAF or a custom filter that blocks this pattern.

